<!--
    A declared __stdcall import's pop is the stack solver's exact equation,
    not an INT_ADD written into the IR before the solve.

    Issue: none filed; found on setuptools' cli-32.exe launcher, where every
    call after GetModuleFileNameA lost its arguments once the import's
    declaration reached the call (`kuna_slotargs`). The solved pops are the
    same in both representations; what changed is how the passes that read the
    stack pointer before the solve see it.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 `ActionExtraPopSetup`
    (`p4_calls/kuna_declaredpop.rs`), read by P6 `StackSolver::build` and its
    push-run guesses (`p6_variables/kuna_calleepop.rs`,
    `p6_variables/kuna_calleepopslot.rs`). A strict fix: no option.

    The fixture `declaredpop_pe_i386.exe` is assembled from
    `declaredpop_pe_i386.asm` (see the fixtures README).

    0x401000 calls GetModuleFileNameA(NULL, path, 0x100), measures `path`
    with a pointer difference, and passes it to an internal __cdecl helper
    whose pop the solver can only guess. With the import's pop as an INT_ADD,
    term collection rewrote the pointer difference in terms of the stack
    pointer at the import's last push, the local-alias boundary took that slot
    as an escaped address, and the helper printed `sub_401080()`.

    0x4010a0 calls VariantClear (declared, pops 8), then `xlen`, an import the
    tables do not declare, on a pushed argument, and takes the stack pointer
    right after it. With VariantClear's pop as an INT_ADD, xlen's push-run
    guess read VariantClear's popped slot as its own (a register save, so
    "pops nothing"), the stack pointer after xlen came out 4 bytes low, and
    the escaped address landed on xlen's argument: `xfill(xlen(),&Stackffffffec)`.

    0x401110, 0x401140 and 0x401170 push an undeclared import's arguments
    right after a declared call, so its push-run guess walks the frame the
    declared call left. A `mov [esp],0x44` local at that stack pointer is not
    a fourth argument of xspawn; EDX's entry value, kept in EDI across the
    call, is xsettime's argument and not a saved register; and xformat's
    third argument is pushed on both sides of a join from GetLastError. A
    wrong count moves the stack pointer after the call, and the function's
    stack argument read after it (`return a2`) becomes a local.

    0x4011a0 and 0x4011d0 do the same with the shapes those readings must
    not take. In GCC's `add esp,12` after a padded call, one of xsettime's
    pushes lands on the stack pointer GetModuleFileNameA left: a push, not a
    `mov [esp]` local, so all four count and `return a1` stays the argument.
    In MinGW's fixed frame, `mov [esp],ebx` writes ECX's entry value as the
    one argument of the __cdecl xopen: no push run to read, so xopen pops
    nothing and the function keeps three parameters.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
